Auto scaling important points 

1. METRICS SERVER IS MANDATORY

Without Metrics Server:
❌ HPA will NOT work.

Install:

helm repo add metrics-server https://kubernetes-sigs.github.io/metrics-server/
helm install metrics-server metrics-server/metrics-server -n kube-system
VERIFY METRICS SERVER
kubectl get apiservices | grep metrics

Expected:

v1beta1.metrics.k8s.io   True
2. CPU REQUESTS ARE MANDATORY

MOST IMPORTANT.

Without:

resources:
  requests:
    cpu:

HPA shows:

cpu: <unknown>

and scaling fails.

3. ALWAYS DEFINE requests AND limits

Enterprise standard:

resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "200m"
    memory: "256Mi"

  limits:
    cpu: "500m"
    memory: "512Mi"
4. replicas SHOULD BE 1

When using HPA:

replicas: 1

Because:

HPA manages scaling automatically.
5. HPA YAML IMPORTANT POINTS

Enterprise HPA:

apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler

metadata:
  name: employee-app-hpa
  namespace: vk67220-ns

spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: employee-app

  minReplicas: 1

  maxReplicas: 5

  metrics:
    - type: Resource

      resource:
        name: cpu

        target:
          type: Utilization

          averageUtilization: 50
6. scaleTargetRef MUST MATCH DEPLOYMENT
name: employee-app

must match:

kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: employee-app
7. HPA WORKS ON UTILIZATION %

Formula:

CPU Utilization=
Requested CPU
Actual CPU Usage
	​

×100

Example:

requested CPU = 200m
actual usage = 400m

Result:

200%
8. VERIFY HPA
kubectl get hpa -n vk67220-ns

Example:

cpu: 120%/50%

Meaning:

current usage = 120%
target = 50%
scale up happening.
9. VERIFY POD METRICS
kubectl top pods -n vk67220-ns
10. VERIFY NODE METRICS
kubectl top nodes
11. SCALE DOWN TAKES TIME

Kubernetes uses:

stabilization window.

So:

scale up fast
scale down slowly.

This is NORMAL.

12. ENTERPRISE SCALE-DOWN CONFIGURATION

Optional:

behavior:
  scaleDown:
    stabilizationWindowSeconds: 60
13. LOAD TESTING IMPORTANT

Simple shell loop:

while true; do :; done

may not always generate enough CPU.

Better:

yes > /dev/null

OR:

stress-ng
ApacheBench
JMeter
k6


